// Deal or Force - strait crossings: narrow water an army walks over.
//
// The province graph is cut wherever the map draws a cell of sea, so without this file
// Tarifa and Tangier are as far apart as Lisbon and New York: a division reaches the other
// shore only aboard a fleet, landed like an invasion. Each entry here joins the province on
// one shore to the province on the other. Marches, supply, fronts, attacks and the AI's
// planning all walk that edge; borders, enclaves and peace settlements do not see it
// (Scripts/Simulation/LandCrossings.cs says why, consumer by consumer).
//
// THE RULE for what belongs here
//   (a) a fixed road or rail link that is open in 2024 - a bridge, a tunnel, a causeway; or
//   (b) a strait about 20 km wide or less at its narrowest, with a regular ferry or a long
//       record of armies and caravans crossing it by boat inside a day.
// Left out on purpose: the Strait of Dover (33 km, and a rail tunnel that shuts the day a
// war opens), Hormuz (39 km), the Palk Strait (30 km, no link), the Taiwan, Bass, Cook,
// Bering and Malacca straits, Sicily-Tunisia, Zanzibar, the Strait of Georgia (a 44 km
// ferry run), and links that are announced and not built (Qatar-Bahrain, Tiran).
//
// Also not here, because this map draws no water there at all and the two shores are
// already neighbours or one province: the Bosphorus and the Dardanelles, the Little Belt
// and the Storstrøm, Rügen, Öland, the Menai Strait and the Solent, Chalkis (Evia), the
// Suez Canal, Djerba, Johor-Singapore, the Bali Strait, Suramadu, Phuket, San Juanico,
// Long Island, the Strait of Canso, the Chacao Channel and the Strait of Magellan.
// An entry for one of those is refused when the map is read, and the log says so.
//
// AN ENTRY
//   "from", "to"   [latitude, longitude] of each shore. Each becomes the land province
//                  nearest that point, within CrossingResolveToleranceKm - never a province
//                  number, which the map compiler is free to change. Put each point clearly
//                  on its own side; the two must be no further apart than CrossingMaxSpanKm.
//   "kind"         bridge | tunnel | causeway | ferry. Decides the cost (below) and whether a
//                  navy can shut it: a ferry does not sail while an enemy fleet commands the
//                  strait; a bridge, tunnel or causeway stays open and is only marked.
//   "name"         the English name, which is also its localisation key.
//   "sea"          optional [latitude, longitude] of the water that commands the crossing,
//                  when that is not simply halfway between the shores.
//   "march"        optional share of march pace kept on this crossing, instead of its kind's.
//   "attack"       optional share of attacking power kept across it, instead of its kind's.
//   "open": false  takes the crossing off the map - how a mod removes one it did not write.
//
// The costs by kind are in common/defines/105_strait_crossings.json. Files in this folder
// are read in name order and later files win key by key: a mod adds a crossing with a new
// key, changes one by naming it again with only the fields it changes, and removes one with
// { "open": false }. An entry that cannot be placed is reported by name in the errors log
// and left out; the rest still load.
//
// To see where every entry landed after an edit:
//   dotnet run -c Release --project Tools/FormableTests -- --crossings-survey
{
  // Europe and the Mediterranean
  "gibraltar_ferry": { "name": "Gibraltar ferry", "kind": "ferry", "from": [36.03, -5.62], "to": [35.82, -5.58] },
